## Evidence and rationale

Comparative studies that evaluate various testing strategies prior to the initiation of antihypertensive treatment were not identified, despite a search of the literature. Therefore, indirect evidence was sought to evaluate this question. An analytic framework was developed to conceptualize the rationale for obtaining diagnostic testing, such as laboratory tests and electrocardiogram (ECG), in this context (see Fig. 2). This framework identified the four most important reasons for obtaining testing, which are to diagnose secondary HTN, identify comorbidities (e.g. DM), identify end organ damage (e.g. chronic kidney disease (CKD) or left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH)), and for cardiac risk stratification.

